  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1229 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nShower/Storm Early this Morning...A broken line of storms has \ndeveloped across northwest WI early this morning ahead of an \neastward advancing cold front. RAP analysis and recent radar \ntrends suggest these storms are elevated and rooted about a stable\nboundary layer. This will limit the severe threat through the \nearly morning hours, however, with a 45-50kt LLJ can't entirely \nrule out a few isolated stronger wind gusts to 30-40mph with any \ncollapsing cells. As storms progress further east across WI they \nwill get further away from the stronger forcing and become more \nisolated. As the cold front works to clear the area this morning \nmay see a brief uptick in storm activity near the lakeshore around\nmid-morning as a narrow corridor of instability builds ahead of \nthe front. Once the front is offshore dry conditions are expected \nfor the rest of today as high pressure builds back into the \nregion. The mostly dry conditions should continue through Sunday \nwith only a low-end (10-20%) chance for scattered showers south of\nHWY 10 Sunday as weak mid-level f-gen develops. \n\nHeavy Rain and Storm Potential Next Week...Ensemble and \ndeterministic models continue to be in fairly good agreement that a \nstrong low pressure system and shortwave will track across the \nnorthern Plains early next week, which will usher in a seasonally \nmoist air mass (PWATs up to 2\", near the 99th percentile of \nclimatology). Increasing WAA associated with a LLJ up on Monday will \nlikely lead to a widespread area of showers and storms  to our \nsouthwest and move into the area Monday afternoon into Tuesday \nmorning, with the exact timing still to be ironed out. Severe \nweather threat looks low due to limited instability and a strong \ncapping inversion, but elevated instability and the mean flow also \nbriefly becomes more parallel with the boundary layer flow which \nwill promote training convection. This could lead to some heavy rain \nand a soaking rain for many spots. Question is just how long it will \nsit over our area and how many rounds of rain any one location will \nsee. NBM probs have lowered a little, down to 30-45% chance for \ngreater than 2\" of rain Monday into early Tuesday, with the highest \nchances across central WI. WPC has also shifted a Slight Chance for \nexcessive rain a little to the south. Will still need to monitor for \nheavy rain with the high PWATs and possible training convection.\n\nRest of the Forecast...Questions remain on how far south a boundary \nwill set up next week within the zonal flow over the Great Lakes and \nif we will hit with additional showers and storms or if high \npressure wins out and we stay mainly dry. NBM brings low end PoPs \n(20-40%), especially across the south, for much of the week, which \nis basically just portraying the differences in location of the \nboundary. Hopefully the picture gets a little clearer as we get \ncloser. If we were to get more bouts of heavy rain later in the \nweek, this could increase the overall flood threat and will need to \nbe monitored. Some cooler air will filter into the region behind the \nsystem on Tuesday, with some chilly air in store if the high wins \nout.",
